<section class="inner-sec" style="padding-top: 10px;">
  <div class="proof-band">
    <div class="sec-eyebrow">Google, ChatGPT, and the rest</div>
    <h2>When someone asks an assistant about your business</h2>
    <p>More people now ask ChatGPT, Google's AI answers, or Perplexity a question instead of scrolling results — "who does emergency furnace repair in Kalispell," "is the barber on Main open Saturday." Something answers them about your business whether you've thought about it or not. The only question is whether it gets you right.</p>
    <div class="proof-points">
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>It's the same work, not a new product</strong>
        <span>Google's own published position is that optimizing for AI search is still SEO. A fast, clearly written site with correct structured data is what feeds both. We don't sell it separately, because it isn't separate.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>Most builder sites are invisible to them</strong>
        <span>The AI crawlers don't run JavaScript. If your site paints itself in the browser — common on drag-and-drop builders — they receive a blank page while you see a full one. We check for that, and every site we build serves its full text and business details to them.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>A wrong answer beats no answer for urgency</strong>
        <span>An assistant confidently giving your old hours or a dead booking link costs you more than not being mentioned. We check what they actually say about you, and tell you the exact question we asked so you can ask it yourself.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="proof-caveat">One honest caveat: AI answers vary by person, location and the day you ask. Anyone selling you an "AI visibility score" is selling a number that changes when someone else asks the same question. We'll show you the exact question we asked and what came back — never a rank.</p>
  </div>
</section>

